<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Rehabilitation hydration principles">
  <div><strong>Access can be the barrier</strong><span>A person may be able to drink but unable to reach, open or hold a container independently.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Swallowing safety comes first</strong><span>Dysphagia can make ordinary liquids unsafe or require a prescribed texture.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Recovery is multidisciplinary</strong><span>Hydration belongs alongside nutrition, mobility, medication review and medical care.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Hydration in rehabilitation</p>
    <h2>The barrier may be access, not thirst</h2>
    <p>Weakness, coordination problems, fatigue, pain, cognition changes or dependence on caregivers can all make ordinary drinking harder. Stroke and neurologic illness can also add swallowing risk.</p>
    <p>The right cup, positioning, assistance, texture and timing may matter more than the brand of drink.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Common barriers</p>
  <h2>Why intake can fall during recovery</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Dysphagia</h3><p>Swallowing impairment may require a specific liquid consistency or supervision.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Mobility and dexterity</h3><p>Weakness, pain or one-sided impairment can make containers hard to use.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Cognition and communication</h3><p>Memory, attention, aphasia or delirium can interfere with drinking.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Medical complexity</h3><p>Fluid restrictions, medications and kidney function can make generic encouragement inappropriate.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p>
    <h2>Only when the oral plan makes it appropriate</h2>
    <p>For someone cleared for the relevant liquid consistency and permitted the ingredients and fluid volume, Best Hydrate may be one beverage option.</p>
    <p>It is not a rehabilitation treatment, dysphagia intervention or substitute for prescribed fluid therapy.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Swallowing safety</span>
    <h3>Do not override a texture prescription.</h3>
    <p>Clinical swallowing recommendations take priority over ordinary beverage preparation.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

